        "start_xml_ordinal": 11670,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 507,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 173,
        "paragraph_id": null,
        "text": "Here  is the Smagorinsky constant (typically ~0.1-0.3) and is the characteristic length scale of the smallest resolvable eddy. The characteristic length scale is essentially the filter width employed and is therefore dependent on the mesh-size. Here the following formulation is used"
      }
    ],
    "following_nonempty_paragraphs": [
      {
        "container_path": "_staging/total-read/model-audit/XBeach/connectivity/nonhydro-read/body-field-recovery/body-cached.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 11772,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 509,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 175,
        "paragraph_id": null,
        "text": "This choice appears to be common in literature for depth averaged flows. It was for instance used in the Boussinesq model by Chen et al. (1999) for the modelling of a Rip Current system."
      },
      {
        "container_path": "_staging/total-read/model-audit/XBeach/connectivity/nonhydro-read/body-field-recovery/body-cached.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 11808,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 511,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 177,
        "paragraph_id": null,
        "text": "Because the magnitude of the eddy viscosity depends on the gradients in the velocity field the Smagorinsky sub-grid model adds very little dissipation in smooth regions of the flow. For wave propagation this is important as in the regions outside the surf zone the influence of (turbulent) viscosity is negligible. On the other hand in the surf zone where large gradients can develop extra dissipation is introduced. This characteristic of the model is considered beneficial, especially in its depth averaged form, as generally energy dissipation due to breaking is underestimated."
      }
    ],
    "table_cell": null,
    "text_method": "Saved display-field substitution plus w:t; equation objects are not transcribed."
  },
